## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a scalable, per-tenant policy engine across REST/GraphQL/gRPC with edge latency constraints and safe rollouts.

## Key Concepts

- Policy language extension (YAML-like)
- Central per-tenant policy registry with TTL
- Edge enforcement using token buckets and Redis-backed state
- Cross-protocol adapters aligned to a canonical EndpointId
- Canary rollouts and safe migration paths

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo policy evaluation sketch
function canServe(tenantId, endpointKey) {
  const bucket = getBucketFromRedis(tenantId, endpointKey);
  if (bucket.tokens > 0) {
    bucket.tokens--;
    return true;
  }
  return false;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you resolve conflicting quotas across overlapping contracts?
- How would you monitor, alert, and roll back policy changes during canaries?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a multi-protocol API platform that bridges REST, GraphQL, and gRPC while handling multi-tenancy, contract versioning, and consistent data mapping for common resources like invoices.

## Key Concepts

- OpenAPI-driven REST surface generation
- GraphQL schema stitching across microservices
- gRPC bridging/translation layer
- Multi-tenancy: tenant_id, per-tenant authentication
- Contract versioning via headers and OpenAPI tags
- Data mapping for resource boundaries (invoice)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: REST handler delegates to GraphQL or gRPC
async function handleInvoiceRequest(req, res) {
  const tenantId = extractTenantFromJWT(req.headers.authorization);
  const version = req.headers['api-version'] || 'v1';

  // Route to appropriate backend based on protocol
  if (req.path.startsWith('/graphql')) {
    return await graphqlResolver(tenantId, req.body.query, version);
  } else if (req.path.startsWith('/grpc')) {
    return await grpcBridge(tenantId, req.method, req.body, version);
  } else {
    return await restHandler(tenantId, req.path, req.body, version);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of cross-protocol idempotency, adapter layering, and a minimal OpenAPI contract in a beginner-friendly setup.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotency keys across REST, GraphQL, gRPC
- Per-protocol adapters translating to a single backend path
- Unified error envelope with code, message, details
- Lightweight in-memory/cache-backed dedup logic

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple backend path for idempotent create
async function createNotification(idempKey, payload) {
  const cached = await redis.get(`notif:${idempKey}`);
  if (cached) return cached;
  const rec = { id: uuid(), ...payload, createdAt: new Date().toISOString() };
  await db.insert(rec);
  await redis.set(`notif:${idempKey}`, rec, 60 * 1000);
  return rec;
}
```

